Microbial communities drive global nutrient cycles, modulate plant growth, and have profound effects on human and animal health. Both the microbes present in a given community and the activities they perform are heavily influenced by their environment. Microbial activity, however, modulates the environment in turn, generating complex feedback structures between microbes and their environment. I seek to understand these feedback structures, which I believe provide deep, fundamental insight into microbial ecology in particular and biological systems in general. To do this, I leverage my background in physics, employing a combination of statistics, experiments, and mathematical models to identify the principles that underlie the relationship between microbial communities and their environments.

